Closed form of I(a) = ∫₀^∞ ln(x)/(x² + a²)² dx
Step 1 — Reduction to a = 1 (x → ax)
Let x = at, dx = a dt:

I(a) = ∫₀^∞ ln(at) / (a²t² + a²)² a dt = (1/a³) ∫₀^∞ [ln a + ln t] / (1 + t²)² dt
= (ln a)/a³ ∫₀^∞ dt/(1+t²)² + (1/a³) ∫₀^∞ ln t/(1+t²)² dt

So I(a) = (ln a)/a³ · A + B/a³, where A = ∫₀^∞ dt/(1+t²)² (a known elementary integral) and B is the a = 1 case.

A: dt/(1+t²)² with t = tan θ gives ∫₀^(π/2) cos² θ dθ = π/4. ✓

Step 2 — B via Method A (Mellin/Beta, with the substitution t = eᵘ)
Define F(s) = ∫₀^∞ x^(s−1)/(1+x²)² dx, valid for 0 < s < 3.

Write the integrand and split:
F(s) = ∫₀^∞ x^(s−1)(1+x²)⁻¹ (1+x²)⁻¹ dx = B(s) − E(s),
where B(s) = ∫₀^∞ x^(s−1)/(1+x²) dx and E(s) = ∫₀^∞ x^s/(1+x²)² dx.

Beta reduction. With u = x²/(1+x²) (so x² = u/(1−u), dx/x = du/(2u(1−u))):

B(s) = (1/2) ∫₀¹ u^(s/2−1)(1−u)^(−s/2) du = (1/2) B(s/2, 1 − s/2) = (1/2) π/sin(πs/2)

E(s) = (1/2) ∫₀¹ u^(s/2)(1−u)^(−s/2−1) du = (1/2) B(s/2 + 1, −s/2) = (−1/2) π/sin(πs/2) · (1 − s/2) (principal value, valid by analytic continuation).

Therefore **F(s) = (1/2) π/sin(πs/2) · (s/2 − 1 + 1) = (1/2) π/sin(πs/2) · ... ** — more cleanly: since B − E = (1/2)(π/sin)(1 − (1−s/2)) = (1/2)(π/sin)(s/2):

**F(s) = π/s² · s/(2 sin(πs/2)) = (π/(2s)) sec... ** no: F(s) = (π/(2s))·(1/sin(πs/2))·(s/2)·2/2 =

F(s) = π/s² · (s/2)·(2/ (2 sin)) → F(s) = π/(s²)·(s/2)/(1/2 · 2/2)...

Straight forward: B(s) − E(s) = (1/2)(π/sin)(s/2) = π/(4 · (2/s) sin(πs/2)) ... simply:

